# Ordinal Addition

The closure ordinal is not a `Nat` wearing a transfinite costume. It is a
Brouwer/tree ordinal — zero, successor, or the limit of a countable
fundamental sequence — and it carries its own arithmetic.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: transfinite ordinal addition;
* phase: closure;
* generating sites: the Brouwer/tree ordinal `Ord`, its order `≤`, the finite
  embedding `ofNat`;
* Lean carrier: `Obsmetrologia.Closure.OrdinalCore`;
* familiar comparison: von Neumann ordinal addition by transfinite recursion
  on the right summand;
* danger: assuming addition is commutative because `Nat.add` is — it is not,
  over genuine ordinals.

The governing sentence is:

> Ordinal addition is generated by recursion on the right summand: it adds
> knowledge at successors and takes the limit of the pointwise sums at
> limits, and it is monotone on both sides but continuous only on the right.

## The Ordinal Itself

`Ord` is the Brouwer/tree ordinal already in play for the closure ordinal:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord}

Its finite fragment is `ofNat`, and `ω` is the limit of all of them:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.ofNat}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.omega}

## Addition By Recursion On The Right

Addition is generated the same way the ordinal itself is: one equation per
constructor of the summand being added.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add}

The three defining equations, named for rewriting:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_zero}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_succ}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_limit}

Checked:

```lean
example (a : Ord) : a + Ord.zero = a := Ord.add_zero a

example (a b : Ord) :
    a + Ord.succ b = Ord.succ (a + b) :=
  Ord.add_succ a b

example (a : Ord) (f : Nat -> Ord) :
    a + Ord.limit f = Ord.limit (fun n => a + f n) :=
  Ord.add_limit a f
```

## Zero Is A Left Identity Too, Not Just Definitional

The right identity `a + 0 = a` is one of the defining equations above. The
left identity is not free — `add` recurses on its *second* argument, so
`0 + a = a` needs its own induction:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.zero_add}

## Associativity

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_assoc}

Checked:

```lean
example (a b c : Ord) : (a + b) + c = a + (b + c) :=
  Ord.add_assoc a b c
```

## Monotone On Both Sides, Continuous Only On The Right

Ordinal addition is famously not commutative: `1 + ω = ω`, but `ω + 1 ≠ ω`.
That asymmetry shows up here as a difference in strength between the two
monotonicity theorems.

Every ordinal sits below its own sum with anything added on the right:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.self_le_add_right}

Addition is monotone in the right argument:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_right_mono}

And monotone — but only monotone, not continuous — in the left argument:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.add_left_mono}

Checked:

```lean
example (a b c : Ord) (h : Ord.Le b c) :
    Ord.Le (a + b) (a + c) :=
  Ord.add_right_mono a h

example (a a' b : Ord) (h : Ord.Le a a') :
    Ord.Le (a + b) (a' + b) :=
  Ord.add_left_mono h b
```

This is why the chapter states monotonicity on the left and right as two
separate theorems rather than one. The right argument's limit case is a
genuine supremum of the pointwise sums (`add_limit` unfolds it to exactly
that limit); the left argument's is not — it is only ever bounded, never
shown to be least.

## Agreement With `Nat.add` On The Finite Fragment

The finite ordinals are closed under addition, and `ofNat` carries the
arithmetic across, not just the order:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.ofNat_add_ofNat}

Checked:

```lean
example (m n : Nat) :
    Ord.ofNat m + Ord.ofNat n = Ord.ofNat (m + n) :=
  Ord.ofNat_add_ofNat m n
```

Together with `Ord.ofNat_mono` (the order embedding), this is the sense in
which our `ℕ` is the finite fragment of `Ord`: not merely order-isomorphic to
it, but arithmetically identified with it.

## `ω + ω` Replaces A Hand-Rolled Successor Tower

`OrdinalCore` used to carry a worked higher limit, `omegaShift`, built by
hand: a hard-coded `Nat.rec` climbing successors on top of `ω`. With real
addition in hand that construction is exactly `ω + ω`, and is defined that
way now:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.omegaShift}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Ord.omegaShift_eq_limit}

Checked:

```lean
example : Ord.omegaShift = Ord.omega + Ord.omega := rfl

example :
    Ord.omegaShift
      = Ord.limit (fun n => Ord.omega + Ord.ofNat n) :=
  Ord.omegaShift_eq_limit
```

The fundamental sequence a hand-written recursion would have had to state
separately — "apply successor to `ω`, `n` times" — is nothing but `n ↦ ω +
n`, read off the general `add` for free.

## Repair Path

If this chapter fails for a reader, use this route:

1. Read `Obsmetrologia.Closure.OrdinalCore` for the ordinal type itself,
   `isFinite`, and the order `≤`.
2. Read this chapter's `Ord.add` for the arithmetic generated on top of it.
3. Read `Obsmetrologia.Closure.Ordinal` for the regime classifier that reads a
   shape off an ordinal — it does not yet consume addition, but shares the
   same carrier.
4. Read `Obsmetrologia.Closure.Ordinal.Finite` for the finite closure ordinal
   over the reading lattice that motivates the whole `Ord` type.

The next closure chapter is the four-way regime classifier built on this
ordinal.
